{"agent_id":"w4c5","agent_seq":5,"timestamp":"2026-08-31T21:54:05.613876293Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"dd1de3f5-0a7b-4f8f-884c-f99285c8831b","comment_uuid":"7dd321ba-f87a-490e-bcad-d120a3e0732b","author":"w4c5","content":"PRE-FLIGHT — VERIFIED REMOTE RECONCILIATION PUBLICATION\n\nNorth star: Crosslink must automatically converge every connected repository on one semantically verified Git authority without a manual migrate/finalize/remigrate sequence, while preserving offline and concurrent work. Binding constraints are lossless Git-native synchronization, idempotent crash recovery, and no writable ready state after incomplete or unverifiable publication. Forbidden patterns are sequential partial publication presented as success, blind force-push, destructive legacy deletion before a commit point, silent network fallback, and migration policy trapped in a CLI command.\n\nLiteral request: On the existing feature branch and draft PR #90 targeting develop, implement the complete prepare/verify/publish/resume/adopt transaction; extract historical readers/builders into reusable reconciler interfaces; retain the old CLI only as a compatibility adapter; prove concurrency, crash, corruption, offline, mixed, and late-legacy behavior locally; do not activate daemon, VS Code, or provider hooks and do not monitor GitHub Actions yet.\n\nCurrent-source reassessment: reconcile.rs only detects formats, plans actions, compares five semantic sections, and reports ReadyCurrent, MigrationRequired, or BlockedCorrupt. commands/migrate_hub_v3.rs builds from mutable cache files, writes canonical v3 refs locally, rolls them back only before publication, then pushes each ref independently. push_v3_refs aggregates failures without making build_and_publish_v3 fail. Adoption fetches current visible refs and hydrates them without a generation commit or journal. hub_v3::push_ref_with_lease protects one ref only. Finalization separately stamps metadata and deletes v2. Existing Git fixtures describe ref names, not object-backed remote histories. PR #90 remains open, draft, merge-clean, and exactly at clean branch tip 7d2ec81.\n\nDivergence risk: A shallow implementation could extract functions and add retry state while preserving multiple mutation authorities and partial remote visibility. That would satisfy the vocabulary but not the promise. Every importer must feed one canonical semantic model, and every publication path must have exactly one authoritative commit point.\n\nChosen approach: Add a reusable historical-source import boundary, semantic verifier, durable atomically-written local journal, and Git publication coordinator under the reconciler. Pin source tips and fingerprints; build immutable migration-scoped target and archive refs; store a generation descriptor containing source evidence, target OIDs, archive OIDs, protocol version, and semantic digest. First attempt lease-guarded atomic multi-ref publication. Fall back only when the remote explicitly lacks atomic-push capability: publish immutable staging/archive refs, then CAS one generation-pointer ref as the authority commit point, resume compatibility-alias materialization from the journal, and never report success until aliases and verification converge. A lease loser fetches the winning descriptor and independently proves semantic equivalence before returning adopted. Unreachable remotes return WaitingForRemote without authority mutation; corrupt or nonequivalent evidence returns BlockedCorrupt with sources and archives intact. The old hub-v3 CLI invokes this engine and no longer owns policy.\n\nAlternative rejected: Refactor migrate_hub_v3.rs in place and improve per-ref retries. It cannot provide one commit point, cannot distinguish a crash from partial success, and cannot prove that a racing winner represents the same source state.\n\nMost likely failure mode: tests pass around a mocked coordinator while real local-bare Git exercises still allow a partial canonical ref set, or fallback is taken for every push error instead of only unsupported atomic capability. A second risk is omitting existing detector families such as local-only and legacy-locks while claiming complete historical import.\n\nRequired evidence: object-backed fixtures for absent/local-only, legacy-locks, v2, hidden-v3, visible-v3, mixed/partial, interrupted, corrupt, and late-legacy states; a real two-clone bare-remote race proving one winner and one independently verified adopter; failpoints before and after every journal, prepare, verify, archive, publish, adopt, and alias transition; assertions that pre-commit failures preserve authoritative refs byte-for-byte and post-commit failures resume to the same generation; current-state no-op proof; remote-unreachable proof; caller survey for every moved or public item; before/after behavior report; cargo build; cargo test including doctests; cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings; cargo fmt --all -- --check. The architect will rerun these independently.\n\nCoordination boundaries: no new dependencies, MSRV change, daemon/readiness/VS Code/provider activation, GitHub push, new PR, PR retarget, or external remote mutation. Tests may mutate only isolated temporary local repositories and bare remotes. Preserve unrelated work and the existing read-only reconcile --check contract.","created_at":"2026-08-31T21:54:05.611143942Z","kind":"plan","signed_by":"SHA256:XnWFB8sWC/arinvVKiYsNYOcKNGxO3Md9d5QkQizAKM","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgvh4fVIEUEUeefLs/7RhSt3fSF7wnc31qS4rmf1e3cRkAAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAg7hdYw7bXVPVZ993CjcEdqwlqJ3RMGPGdEPZEi8h5bniXZblSTszWCxcvcsqRM8vMkcPIK0kUQ9zzB0XyAlvCg=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:XnWFB8sWC/arinvVKiYsNYOcKNGxO3Md9d5QkQizAKM","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgvh4fVIEUEUeefLs/7RhSt3fSF7wnc31qS4rmf1e3cRkAAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQAtYliylC9eLzqfHy3442k47Rp6LwjYnbqSnjeiL5CS5sbTmbqCL0HVQWbg8uHeDMGKt6hrPgrVUlR6mmghoRwA="}
